  title: 'Medical Error Disclosure — Wrong-Side Injection',
  url: '',
  sections: [
    Section(
      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content:
          'You are a physiatrist in an outpatient interventional spine clinic. You just completed a fluoroscopy-guided sacroiliac (SI) joint injection on a 52-year-old female with chronic low back pain and left-sided SI joint dysfunction confirmed on physical examination and diagnostic block. The procedure went technically well with good contrast flow pattern and the patient tolerated it without complications. As you are completing the procedure note, you review the original order and realize the injection was ordered and consented for the **right** SI joint, but you performed it on the **left** SI joint. You look back at the clinical notes and see that the patient\'s pain was originally documented as **right-sided** by the referring physician 2 months ago, but your own examination 1 week ago documented **left-sided** tenderness and positive provocative testing on the left. The patient is still in the recovery area. She has not yet been told about the discrepancy.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content:
          '**Patient Background:** 52-year-old female, chronic low back pain x 3 years attributed to bilateral SI joint dysfunction (right > left per referring provider, left > right per your exam). History of lumbar fusion L4-L5 (5 years ago) with adjacent segment degeneration. Works as an elementary school teacher. On gabapentin 300 mg TID and meloxicam 15 mg daily.\n\n**Procedure Details:**\n- Order: Right SI joint injection (placed by your clinic based on referring physician\'s laterality)\n- Consent: Signed for right SI joint injection\n- Pre-procedure verification: Time-out was performed but the discrepancy between the order (right) and your exam findings (left) was not caught\n- Procedure performed: Left SI joint injection under fluoroscopic guidance — technically successful, appropriate needle placement confirmed with contrast\n- Medications injected: 1 mL 0.25% bupivacaine + 40 mg triamcinolone\n\n**Immediate Assessment:**\n- Patient is hemodynamically stable, no procedural complications\n- The injection itself was performed safely and correctly from a technical standpoint\n- The left SI joint WAS clinically symptomatic on YOUR examination\n- However: the consent, the order, and the referring physician\'s laterality all specified the right side\n- The patient may actually benefit from the left-sided injection (your exam supported left-sided pathology)\n- The patient still needs the right-sided injection per the original treatment plan\n\n**Key Complexity:** This is not a clear-cut "wrong side" error in the traditional sense — there is clinical justification for the left-sided injection. However, it was not the procedure that was ordered or consented for, making it a deviation from the planned care regardless of clinical rationale.',
