    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING',
      content:
          '**Adverse Event Classification:**\n- This is a **wrong-site procedural error** per The Joint Commission and CMS definitions, regardless of clinical justification\n- Classified as a "Never Event" (National Quality Forum Serious Reportable Event) — procedures performed on the wrong body part\n- The fact that the injected side was clinically appropriate does not change the classification — the procedure did not match the consent or the order\n- This is a **near-miss with patient contact** — harm was minimal (the injection was safe and potentially therapeutic) but the systems failure is significant\n\n**Patient Harm Evaluation:**\n- **Physical harm:** Minimal to none. The injection was performed safely on a clinically symptomatic joint. No adverse effects from the medication. The patient received a therapeutically appropriate intervention.\n- **Procedural harm:** The patient underwent a procedure she did not specifically consent to. She still needs the originally planned right-sided injection — an additional procedure and additional risk exposure.\n- **Trust harm:** The most significant potential harm is to the patient-physician relationship if disclosure is handled poorly or delayed.\n\n**Root Cause Analysis (Preliminary):**\n1. **Order entry:** Medical assistant entered laterality from the referral rather than from your examination note. No verification step between clinician assessment and order entry.\n2. **Consent process:** Consent was generated from the order, propagating the laterality error. Neither the patient nor the physician caught the discrepancy during the consent discussion.\n3. **Time-out failure:** The pre-procedure time-out read the consent form but did not independently verify laterality with the proceduralist\'s clinical intent. The time-out was procedural (checkbox) rather than substantive.\n4. **Cognitive factors:** You (the proceduralist) had the correct clinical plan in your head but did not verify it matched the paperwork. Confirmation bias — you assumed the paperwork matched your intent.\n5. **System design:** No hard stop between order laterality and proceduralist\'s documented plan.',
