    Section(
      title: '+ Diagnostic question',
      content:
          '**Q: Is this truly a medical error if the injection was clinically indicated on the left side based on your examination? Does the clinical appropriateness change the reporting obligation?**\n\n**A:** Yes, this is a medical error, and no, the clinical appropriateness does NOT change the reporting obligation:\n\n1. **Ethical analysis:** The patient consented to a right-sided injection and received a left-sided injection. Informed consent is a legal and ethical requirement for any invasive procedure. Performing a different procedure than what was consented for is a violation of patient autonomy, regardless of whether the performed procedure was clinically beneficial.\n\n2. **Legal analysis:** Wrong-site procedures are "never events" per CMS and TJC. They must be reported through institutional channels and to the state health department (in most states). Clinical justification is a mitigating factor in potential liability but does not eliminate the reporting requirement.\n\n3. **Regulatory analysis:** CMS Conditions of Participation require reporting of all never events. Failure to report is a separate violation and could jeopardize the facility\'s CMS certification.\n\n4. **Nuance for this case:** In peer review and any subsequent legal proceedings, the fact that you had a documented clinical rationale for the left-sided injection is highly relevant. Your pre-procedure clinic note supports left-sided pathology. This shifts the root cause from a random wrong-site error to a **systems failure in order reconciliation** — a more defensible position, but still requiring full disclosure and reporting.\n\n5. **Personal learning:** The correct course of action would have been to recognize the order/consent discrepancy BEFORE the procedure, correct the order, re-consent for the left side, and then proceed. Going forward, this becomes a personal practice pattern — always verify that the consent matches your clinical intent, not just that a consent exists.',
