    Section(
      title: '+ Challenge question',
      content:
          '**Q: Your medical assistant, who entered the incorrect order, is visibly upset and says, "This is my fault. Am I going to be fired?" How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** This is an opportunity to model a just culture response:\n\n"First, I want to tell you that this is NOT solely your fault. Multiple people — including me — had the opportunity to catch this discrepancy, and none of us did. I am the one who performed the procedure without verifying the consent matched my clinical plan. The ultimate responsibility is mine.\n\nHere is how I think about this: you entered the order based on the referral, which is a reasonable thing to do. The problem is that we do not have a system in place to verify that the order matches my updated clinical assessment. That is a system gap, not a personal failure.\n\nYou are not going to be fired for a systems error. What I need from you is two things:\n1. Be honest in the incident report about what happened — exactly as it occurred, without embellishment or minimization.\n2. Help me design a better process so this cannot happen again — you understand the workflow better than anyone.\n\nIn a just culture, we do not punish people for honest errors within a flawed system. We punish reckless behavior. Entering an order from a referral is not reckless. If anything, the system failed you by not requiring a verification step."\n\n**Important distinctions in just culture:**\n- **Human error (this case):** Inadvertent action, slip, or mistake in a system with inadequate safeguards. Response: system redesign, not punishment.\n- **At-risk behavior:** Conscious deviation from safe practice (e.g., skipping time-out). Response: coaching, not punishment.\n- **Reckless behavior:** Conscious disregard of substantial risk (e.g., performing a procedure while impaired). Response: disciplinary action.\n\nThis MA\'s action was human error in a system without adequate checks. The appropriate response is to fix the system.',
