    Section(
      title: '+ Management question',
      content:
          '**Q: At the 2-week follow-up, the patient reports 80% pain relief from the left-sided injection. She says, "I guess you injected the right side after all — the one that was hurting." How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** This is a critical moment — the temptation to let the misunderstanding stand is significant but must be resisted:\n\n"Actually, I want to clarify something important. I injected your left SI joint, not your right. As I explained to you on the day of the procedure, there was a discrepancy between the original order for the right side and what I found on my examination, which pointed to the left side as the more symptomatic joint.\n\nThe good news is that the left-sided injection has clearly helped you — 80% relief is an excellent result. This confirms that the left SI joint was indeed a significant pain generator, which is consistent with what I found on your exam.\n\nNow, the question is whether we still need to address the right SI joint. Based on how you are feeling, let me re-examine you today and we can make that decision together."\n\n**Why this matters:**\n- Letting the patient believe the right side was injected would compound the original error with a deception\n- If the right side is never injected and pain returns, the patient would be confused about her own treatment history\n- Accurate patient understanding of their treatment is essential for future medical decision-making\n- Transparency, even when uncomfortable, builds long-term trust\n\n**Clinical decision:** If she has 80% relief from the left injection alone, the right side may not need injection at this time. Reassess clinically and only inject the right side if residual right-sided symptoms warrant it. This case may demonstrate that the referring physician\'s laterality was incorrect and the physiatrist\'s examination was more accurate — useful information for the patient\'s long-term pain management plan.',
