    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE',
      content:
          '**Institutional Error Reporting:**\n\n**1. Internal Reporting (Immediate):**\n- File an institutional incident/safety event report through the hospital\'s electronic reporting system\n- Classify as a wrong-site procedure (Never Event)\n- Include all contributing factors identified in root cause analysis\n- Notify department chief/medical director\n- Notify risk management\n\n**2. Patient Safety Organization (PSO) Reporting:**\n- If the institution participates in a federally designated PSO, this event should be reported through the PSO channel\n- PSO reporting provides legal privilege and confidentiality protections for quality improvement activities under the Patient Safety and Quality Improvement Act of 2005\n- PSO analysis aggregates wrong-site events across institutions to identify common system failures\n\n**3. State Reporting:**\n- Most states require mandatory reporting of Never Events to the state health department\n- Reporting timelines vary (typically 5-7 business days after discovery)\n- Failure to report is a separate regulatory violation\n\n**4. CMS Reporting:**\n- CMS requires reporting of Never Events as a condition of participation\n- CMS does not reimburse for care related to Never Events — the facility absorbs the cost of the original procedure and any corrective procedures\n\n**Malpractice Risk Management:**\n- **Early disclosure reduces litigation risk:** Studies consistently show that transparent, empathetic disclosure reduces malpractice claims by 30-40% compared to concealment or defensive responses\n- **Contact professional liability insurer:** Notify your malpractice carrier within 24 hours of the event. This is typically a policy requirement.\n- **Documentation principles:** Document factually. Record what happened, when it was discovered, what the patient was told, and what corrective actions were taken. Do NOT use words like "error," "negligence," or "fault" in the medical record — use descriptive language. Reserve analysis for the privileged incident report.\n- **Apology laws:** Many states have "apology laws" (I\'m Sorry laws) that protect expressions of empathy and apology from being used as evidence of liability. Know your state\'s law.\n- **Risk of concealment:** NOT disclosing the error is far more legally dangerous than disclosing. If discovered later (and it will be — through billing, records review, or the patient\'s own observation), concealment transforms a defensible error into potential fraud.\n\n**Quality Improvement:**\n- Present de-identified case at department morbidity and mortality (M&M) conference\n- Develop laterality verification protocol applicable to all injection procedures\n- Audit compliance with enhanced time-out process quarterly\n- Consider simulation training for the procedural team incorporating laterality verification scenarios',
