    Section(
      title: '+ Systems question',
      content:
          '**Q: A colleague who learns about the event says, "I would never disclose that. The patient benefited from the injection. Telling her just opens you up to a lawsuit for no reason." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** This advice, though well-intentioned, is ethically and practically wrong:\n\n1. **Ethical obligation:** The AMA Code of Medical Ethics (Opinion 8.6) explicitly states that physicians must disclose adverse events and medical errors to patients. This is not optional. The patient has a right to know what was done to her body, especially when it differed from what she consented to.\n\n2. **Legal reality:** Non-disclosure is far more legally dangerous than disclosure. If the patient discovers the discrepancy through billing records, future imaging, or another provider\'s review, the concealment becomes evidence of consciousness of guilt. A malpractice case built on a concealed error is exponentially more damaging than one built on a disclosed error with corrective action.\n\n3. **Evidence base:** The Michigan model and similar programs demonstrate that **proactive disclosure plus early offer of resolution reduces malpractice claims, litigation costs, and defense expenses**. CRICO (Harvard\'s malpractice insurer) data shows that failure to communicate is a contributing factor in over 30% of malpractice cases.\n\n4. **Institutional mandate:** Most hospitals have mandatory disclosure policies for adverse events. Concealing a reported incident would be a separate policy violation.\n\n5. **Personal integrity:** "I understand your instinct to protect me, and I appreciate it. But I could not look this patient in the eye at her follow-up visit knowing I concealed something from her. If I cannot be honest with my patients when I make a mistake, I have no business asking them to trust me with their care.\n\nThe relationship I have with my patients is built on trust. One injection on the wrong side will not destroy that trust — but hiding it would."\n\n6. **Systemic concern:** If the colleague\'s advice reflects institutional culture, this represents a deeper patient safety problem that should be raised with quality leadership.',
