    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          '**Scenario:** You walk into the recovery area where the patient is resting comfortably after the procedure. She smiles and says, "That was not bad at all. I hope it helps. When can I go back to work?" You need to disclose the wrong-site error.\n\n**Honest, Empathetic Disclosure Without Defensive Language:**\n\n**Step 1 — Sit down and establish presence:** Do not hover or appear rushed. Sit at the patient\'s eye level. Make eye contact. Your body language must communicate that this is important and you are giving it your full attention.\n\n**Step 2 — Begin with what happened, simply and clearly:**\n"Before we talk about your recovery, I need to share something important with you. I want to be completely transparent.\n\nDuring the procedure today, I injected your left SI joint. However, the order and your consent form specified the right SI joint. I discovered this discrepancy as I was completing the paperwork after the procedure."\n\n**Step 3 — Pause and let her process.** Do not rush to fill the silence.\n\n**Step 4 — Explain the context without making excuses:**\n"Here is the full picture: when I examined you last week, your left side was clearly the more symptomatic joint — the side causing you the most pain. That is the side I intended to inject and the side I did inject. However, the original referral specified the right side, and that is what was entered in the order and on the consent form. I did not catch the discrepancy between my clinical plan and the written documentation before the procedure. That is my responsibility."\n\n**Step 5 — Acknowledge the error directly:**\n"This should not have happened. Regardless of the clinical reasoning, you consented to a right-sided injection and received a left-sided injection. I am sorry this occurred."\n\n**Step 6 — Address safety and next steps:**\n"The good news is that the left-sided injection was performed safely and is likely to help your pain based on my examination findings. You are not in any danger. However, you still may need the right-sided injection as originally planned. I would like to schedule that in two to three weeks, and we will go through the entire consent and verification process again with enhanced safeguards.\n\nI have also reported this to our quality and safety team so we can improve our procedures to prevent this from happening to any patient in the future."\n\n**Step 7 — Invite questions and emotions:**\n"I know this is not what you expected to hear. You have every right to be upset, concerned, or to ask me any questions. How are you feeling about this?"\n\n**Language to AVOID:**\n- "It was technically the correct side anyway" (minimizing)\n- "The system failed" (deflecting personal responsibility)\n- "This never happens" (dismissive and likely untrue)\n- "You should be fine" (premature reassurance)\n- "It was just a misunderstanding" (downplaying)\n- "The referral was wrong" (blaming others)',
