    Section(
      title: '+ Communication question',
      content:
          '**Q: The patient becomes angry and says, "How can I trust you to do the right side if you could not even do the correct side the first time? I want a different doctor." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** Accept the response without defensiveness:\n\n"Your reaction is completely understandable, and you have every right to feel this way. If the situation were reversed, I would feel the same. Trust is earned, and I understand that my actions today have damaged yours.\n\nIf you would like a different physician to perform the right-sided injection, I will make that happen. I can refer you to my colleague Dr. [name], who is equally qualified, and I will provide a complete summary of your case so there is no gap in your care. Your comfort and confidence in your physician are essential.\n\nIf, after some time, you decide you are willing to give me another chance, I want you to know what I have already done to prevent this from ever happening again: we are implementing a new laterality verification protocol where I personally confirm the side before any order is entered, and we are adding a patient-confirmation step where I will ask you to point to the side we are treating before we begin. These changes will be in place before your next procedure, regardless of which physician performs it.\n\nEither way, I want to assure you of two things: first, the injection you received today was on a joint that my examination showed was symptomatic, so it may actually help your pain. Second, I will continue to be available if you have any questions or concerns, even if you choose to see a different physician going forward.\n\nIs there anything else you would like to know or anything else I can do for you right now?"\n\n**Key principles in this response:**\n- Do not argue or try to change her mind\n- Do not take her anger personally or respond defensively\n- Offer concrete alternatives (different physician) without making her feel she is overreacting\n- Demonstrate corrective action already taken\n- Leave the door open for the relationship to recover\n- Maintain focus on her needs, not your feelings\n- Do not grovel or over-apologize — that shifts the emotional labor to the patient to reassure YOU',
